"images/54/542195ea9617c2d674bb5a96a116033db8204114a5c8ba44a164c82b6133c552.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126918 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126918 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b16f1085-6b45-5b90-8c6d-7f7be91cd536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126919 #edb/ref 1080 "images/03/03871a7ce58953f8cca6239641e0b18b7a4fcaed50fbed781c097ce2acbc2f08.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126919 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126919 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10f104f2-cc54-5f5c-a53b-614166d0293b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126920 #edb/ref 1080 "images/2f/2fe937dda01135a13db51ef5a3b5f924f0e53aab59e4fe583a2a55bac753d995.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126920 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126920 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "119a15c5-2653-58d3-9dfe-83a5f6f95715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126921 #edb/ref 1080 "images/da/dac93a6d36f27b7c9eeb6640370d3736fa8353ee12697721a8ab1b416915dbef.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "17439721-eac2-5a3e-89b7-5307977ce0ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126922 #edb/ref 1080 "images/3d/3df55302db431e7e2bc937028b1737956ae73299b59cf07be8e7b2fa02d2b87a.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126922 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186126922 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a1e691e-5f3e-549d-b797-a753fd439226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128955 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128955 #edb/ref 1071 "Sketch the curve $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{5}(x-4)(x^2 + 5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75d559bd-99c1-5374-9ae6-0096a8b1d86e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128955 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $x^2 + 5 = 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 0^2 - 4(1)(5) = -20 < 0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{5}(0-4)({0}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(-4)(5) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,-4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b8d12adf0f04b7c1d9d05271c9378b941cf107d713932055a1fc48371952a1c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128956 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128956 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 - 4x - 24,$ sketch the curve $y = f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ebf167f-0c61-5721-9019-1da86d591c7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128956 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & -4 & -24 \\\\  &  & 3 & 12 & 24 \\\\  & 1 & 4 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f(x)}{x-3} = x^2 + 4x + 8,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)(x^2 + 4x + 8).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $x^2 + 4x + 8 = 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 4^2 - 4(1)(8) = -16 <                    0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}-4(0)-24=-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-24),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/fa/fa1af56b5acbb196567d5943db53cf34405ab59cdb5e73a8a9fc8021d2864373.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128957 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128957 #edb/ref 1071 "Sketch the curve $f(x) = -(x-2)(x^2 + 2x + 2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acf8ac05-4080-50ea-8a33-dca4a08cb7e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128957 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $x^2 + 2x + 2 = 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}= 2^2 - 4(1)(2) = -4 <  0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0-2)({0}^{2}+2(0)+2) \\\\  & =-(-2)(2) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/1b/1bbbfefc0260959a5008b5b4597293e0d4acdeb390601cfdb53a8d700ffbbf54.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128959 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128959 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x) = -x^3 + 4x^2 - 4x + 16,$ sketch the curve $y=f(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50dda1cf-e61a-5efb-9671-a66d71360b84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128959 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & -4 & 16 \\\\  &  & -4 & 0 & -16 \\\\  & -1 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x-4} & =-{x}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x) = -(x-4)(x^2 + 4).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $x^2 + 4 = 0$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = 0^2 - 4(1)(4) = - 16  <     0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny = -(0)^3 + 4(0)^2 - 4(0) + 16 = 16\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,16),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows (figure not to scale):\n\n![](images/aa/aa0418567de10fa6bd229a3d1bfbff2a71f242232f8af8dd001b096ecc9c8038.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1039 "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129140 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "40733109-8985-5f30-9678-289216c1da00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129141 #edb/ref 1039 "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Positive Leading Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129141 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129141 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4ef84d8b-a825-581a-aa43-2b18b42ab882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129143 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129143 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129143 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129143 #edb/ref 1039 "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129143 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129143 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fde30544-dd02-5df3-838f-896d81562ebb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129146 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129146 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129146 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186410418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129146 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129146 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186558557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129146 #edb/ref 1039 "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with a Negative Leading Coefficient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129146 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129146 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5fd988de-d8c3-5dcb-b3c3-636d4173d5de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1045 "Graphing Cubic Curves Containing One Distinct Real Root" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186129988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129396 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bb4f4046-3730-5392-be21-d66c1f2457af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129572 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129572 #edb/ref 1069 "q-28196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129572 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129572 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(2-x)({x}^{2}+3x+3)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129572 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4af252a3-6966-5006-8a08-9a500d4173b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129572 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3x+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(3)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-3<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=2.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(2-0)({0}^{2}+3(0)+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)(3)}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,2),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=2,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/da/dac93a6d36f27b7c9eeb6640370d3736fa8353ee12697721a8ab1b416915dbef.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129713 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129713 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129713 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129713 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+8{x}^{2}+7x+3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129713 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f032169-ef22-5e6b-92e9-31b38bfaf554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129713 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 8 & 7 & 3 \\\\  &  & -6 & -6 & -3 \\\\  & 2 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x+3}=2{x}^{2}+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+3)(2{x}^{2}+2x+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(1)=-4<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)^{3}+8(0)^{2}+7(0)+3=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f004d901b5738a46732f41c12f12c812432a2fa14af71469b5abff49e368607d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129988 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129988 #edb/ref 1061 21027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129988 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "051afa25-c36b-5ee0-9d9d-eb0b733f3ede" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129988 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129989 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129989 #edb/ref 1061 21029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129989 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb11761e-2cec-5933-a69a-8624ea5f99b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129989 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129990 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129990 #edb/ref 1061 21032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129990 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "865f0324-f2f1-593d-a63c-922f8ee140ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129990 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129991 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129991 #edb/ref 1061 21033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129991 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d1f3375e-451a-5569-acec-c0bde4d976fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129991 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129994 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129994 #edb/ref 1061 21037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129994 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25f7fb47-95e9-58de-9b40-bdc5ea29bc79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129994 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129999 #edb/ref 1061 21043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129999 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6e25844-0c2f-5a68-af2d-f3b6ac122e86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129999 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130361 #edb/ref 1064 "The graph of any cubic function $y = f(x)= ax^3 + bx^2 + cx +d,$ where $a >0,$ has a characteristic inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape, with $y$-values ranging from $-\\infty$ to $+\\infty.$ This means that every such function has at least one real root and at most three distinct roots. \n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to plot the graph of $y = x^3-3x^2 +3x-1.$ First, we need to find the roots, so we set $y=0$ and solve. Noting that the polynomial is a perfect cube, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+3x-1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get one real root $x=1,$ which has multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAt a root of multiplicity $3,$ the graph becomes **tangent** (i.e., lies parallel) to the $x$-axis and crosses the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d8/d87834ef3f7225b00d92e6051a67735cf6695c5a2eff9635c4de416d0be1577f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130361 #edb/ref 1065 3752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130361 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "848b066b-7e36-58cf-ae75-a8e28e9d3358" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130361 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130364 #edb/ref 1064 "The graph of any cubic function $y = f(x)= ax^3 + bx^2 + cx +d,$ where $a <0,$ has a characteristic ${\\textsf{S}}$-shape, with $y$-values ranging from $+\\infty$ to $-\\infty.$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we want to plot the graph of $y = -x^3+3x^2 -3x+1.$ First, we need to find the roots, so we set $y=0$ and solve. Noting that the polynomial is a perfect cube, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ (-x+1)^{3} & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)^{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get one real root $x=1,$ which has multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAt a root of multiplicity $3,$ the graph becomes tangent to the $x$-axis a