58121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558461 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186558329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558461 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558461 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558461 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186558329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558461 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d704154a-6d5d-5adf-9ea8-05adad19a091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558481 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}-3x-9,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "07788a4c-c93b-51a6-bd5f-2540eb30230f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558481 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & -4 & -12 & -3 & -9 \\\\  &  & 12 & 0 & 9 \\\\  & -4 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+3} & =-4{x}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =-(4{x}^{2}+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+3)(4{x}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $4{x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(4)(3)=-48<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-4(0)^{3}-12(0)^{2}-3(0)-9=-9\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-9),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d05449fcf2c39749736b58099a32f59ff85566d5ea56d0e4ec61e88da8ce7aa.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75187" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558488 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}+3)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1114090a-5268-5e5c-ab01-061822ccfb1c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558488 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(1)(3)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-4)({0}^{2}+3) \\\\  & =(-4)(3) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,-12),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/31/310ba3049a0a91f23259a67eb3c0606749f6a83a75151d6b0d41dfc4e98706d1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558536 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558536 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112435" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558536 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558536 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)=3{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+x-3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8e99018d-106a-536c-a32e-4bcfd5c27a01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558536 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x-3)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 3 & 3 & -9 & 1 & -3 \\\\  &  & 9 & 0 & 3 \\\\  & 3 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(x)}{x-3}=3{x}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-3)(3{x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $3{x}^{2}+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(3)(1)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3(0)^{3}-9(0)^{2}+0-3=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=3,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7a/7a04ed5e26758551d7eca9949a274884f28f43a001439b58e393470721d96e5d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-28197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558543 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}(4-x)({x}^{2}+6)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99119256-67b3-5344-8812-e11eceeb5da0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558543 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of ${x}^{2}+6=0$ is \n\n$$\nD={0}^{2}-4(1)(6)=-24<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{8}(4-0)({0}^{2}+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(6)}{8} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/79/795ec06d95058de55d870db3a7559f44277003afdfd5ce1231493abd520404e2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558557 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558557 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558557 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558557 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-2{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-5x-3,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558557 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b580f52d-26f6-5405-9c2b-c27d4700d6b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558557 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. We are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so $(x+1)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder. To begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -2 & -4 & -5 & -3 \\\\  &  & 2 & 2 & 3 \\\\  & -2 & -2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f(x)}{x+1} & =-2{x}^{2}-2x-3 \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{2}+2x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-(x+1)(2{x}^{2}+2x+3).\n$$\n\nThe equation is now fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $2{x}^{2}+2x+3=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(2)^{2}-4(2)(3)=-20<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-1.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by substituting the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\ny=-2(0)^{3}-4(0)^{2}-5(0)-3=-3\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at $(0,-3),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-1,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the graph has an $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d391911307bc35f7481fe8b3a0b3871d1c4b75cc44d4459018fe00b14133f4f9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558575 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558575 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558575 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558575 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x+4)(3{x}^{2}+1)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558575 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f891d27b-7abc-5935-b2a7-003a7da88708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558575 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation is already fully factored. Note that the discriminant of $3{x}^{2}+1=0$ is \n\n$$\nD=(0)^{2}-4(3)(1)=-12<0,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that the quadratic factor has no real roots. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the function $f(x)$ has a single root at $x=-4.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+4)(3(0)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(4)(1) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the curve:\n\n- has a $y$-intercept at the point $(0,4),$\n\n- passes through the $x$-axis at the single root $x=-4,$ and\n\n- has no more $x$-intercepts.\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the graph takes an inverted $\\text{S}$-shape. Therefore, the graph looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fa/fa8a5e889a374aae31d7cf7dd82581b00c4d74abfc08b14721f1da3ea19b2e95.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
